NARR = [
"In the ADS1299 diagram, there's no adder and no divider, and yet the bias amplifier receives an average. So how does that value come about? Let's work it out with simple numbers, step by step.",
"Start with the simplest case: two sources, 10 volts and 20 volts, each connected through a resistor of the same size to one shared wire. What voltage settles on that wire? Nothing else is attached to it, so no current can pile up there. Whatever current flows in from the 20 volt side must flow out to the 10 volt side. Current through a resistor is the voltage difference divided by R. So, 20 minus V, over R, equals V minus 10, over R. The R cancels. 20 minus V equals V minus 10, so V is 15 volts. Exactly the average of 10 and 20.",
"Now three sources: 10, 12 and 8 volts. Again the currents into the wire must add up to zero. 10 minus V, plus 12 minus V, plus 8 minus V, all over R, equals zero. That gives 30 minus 3 V equals zero, so V is 10 volts. In general, with n sources, V is the sum of the voltages divided by n. The resistors do the adding, and the number of branches does the dividing.",
"This only works because the resistors are equal. Make one resistor half the size, and that source pulls twice as hard. The wire moves toward it, to 9.5 volts. Equal resistors mean every source counts equally. Inside a chip, resistors made side by side on the same silicon match each other very closely, so this trick works well.",
"Now the switches. A switch that is open carries no current, so that source is simply left out. Open the third switch, and only 10 and 12 remain: the wire moves to 11 volts. In the ADS1299, these switches are the bias sense registers, one bit for every P and every N input. The OpenBCI GUI's bias setting for each channel controls them.",
"What are the sources? For each channel, the two inputs of the amplifier. One input is the shared reference, and the other is your scalp electrode. Which side is which depends on the setup: the Cyton puts the reference on the P side, using SRB2, while TI's own referential example puts it on the N side, using SRB1. Both inputs carry the same mains hum, which is big. Ideally the reference carries the hum alone, and the scalp electrode carries the hum plus your brain signal. Their average is the hum plus half of the brain signal. But the brain signal is thousands of times smaller than the hum, so what's left is almost pure hum. The difference between the two inputs is the part we want: it's exactly the EEG we record.",
"Each channel's hum is a little different, depending on where the electrode sits. Averaging across all the switched-on channels gives a good estimate of the hum on your body, and brain signals, which differ from place to place, average out even further.",
"That average goes into the minus input of the bias amplifier, while the plus input sits at a reference level. So the amplifier's output is the hum turned upside-down. It leaves through BIAS OUT and the resistor R5, into your body through the bias electrode, and it pushes the hum back down. Less hum on your body means cleaner signals on every channel.",
"To sum up. Currents into the shared wire must add to zero. With equal resistors, that makes its voltage the plain average. The switches choose who takes part. The amplifier flips the result, and drives it back into your body.",
]
